<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhadrachalam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhadrachalam is a temple town on the left bank of the Godavari river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadradri-kothagudem-district">Bhadradri Kothagudem district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is best known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> Ramachandraswamy temple, a major pilgrimage centre dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, rebuilt in the 17th century by Kancherla Gopanna, remembered as Bhakta Ramadasu. The town lies in a forested region with a large Koya tribal population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam.jpg" alt="Bhadrachalam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WithShivaram · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_sita_rama_temple_bhadrachalam_temple_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
